## 📌 Alert Model & Key Fields (what L1 must read first)

The lab emphasizes consistently reviewing these properties before deep investigation:
- Time: When the alert was created (often delayed vs event time).
- Name: Detection-rule summary of suspected behavior.
- Severity: Urgency/impact score (often set by detection engineering).
- Status: Indicates whether an alert is being worked (ex: In Progress) or done (Closed).
- Verdict (Classification): True Positive / False Positive decision.
- Assignee: Ownership of investigation.
- Description: Context + what detection logic expects.
- Alert Fields: Evidence/indicators such as host, user, IP, command line.

---

## 🔎 Prioritization Logic Used (L1 Queue Handling)

The room’s recommended prioritization model:
1. Filter alerts: only take new/unseen/unassigned alerts.
2. Sort by severity: critical → high → medium → low.
3. Sort by time: start with oldest first (older threats may already be causing damage).

Validation questions in the lab reflected these rules:
- Prioritize medium over low? → Yea.
- Take newest first then older? → Nay.

